00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:26.170
GANG CHEN: OK, I hope you
all had a good spring break

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:29.380
and are refreshed.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.930
I spent three days
in DC, brainstorming.

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:35.060
It was pretty good.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:41.330
So if you recall in the week
before our last lecture,

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:45.790
we were discussing
the wave propagation

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:47.920
in layered structures.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:54.100
And we spent time in
deriving the reflectance--

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.560
reflection coefficient
if I have one layer.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.560
And there are three strategies.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:04.510
One, physically, what happens
if I have wave comes in?

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:06.640
The first time the wave
meets the interface,

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:07.570
there's reflection.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:08.650
There's transmission.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:12.890
Go to the last interface,
reflection, transmission.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:17.710
So there are an infinite number
of reflection and transmission.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.720
And of course, if
there are absorption

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.370
during the propagation of
the wave in the layers,

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:27.490
you will-- the
intensity will be lost.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:29.980
Otherwise, it's
say-- we wrote down,

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:37.340
the strategy is, you can always
do the plan wave propagation.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.520
And if you can ride
the plan wave form,

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.920
you can sum up and do
an infinite series.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.960
So we didn't do that in class.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.200
But that's what the physics is.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.060
And what we spent time is
this so-called resultant wave

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.020
method.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.520
We say, OK, let's group
this forward propagating

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.900
which has the same phase
factor all into one term.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:05.110
Except, now we don't have
fixed relation between coming

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.330
and out, say, in the
full propagating,

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:11.020
because, you can say,
from the physics there,

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:13.540
it's multiple additions, right?

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:15.580
So we want to find
the coefficients.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.310
And similarly, backward
propagating and we

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:21.670
use the continuity of
the Maxwell equation

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:22.780
at the boundary.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:28.400
And we derive the expression
for the reflection coefficient.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.020
So that's what we spent time on.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:36.220
And the third method, which
is a much more general,

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.810
it's the transfer-matrix method.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:42.490
And here, you can say, we're
still distinguishing forward,

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:44.050
backward propagating.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:46.660
And we're doing
electrical field.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:51.040
And transfer-matrix
method is writing down

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.880
both incident reflected
all group into,

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.370
what's the electric
field component?

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:59.710
What's the magnetic
field component?

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:04.190
And because, at the interface,
these are continuous,

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:08.350
so it's easy for me to propagate
this through many layers,

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:13.660
as long as I have the
transfer-matrix, right?

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.700
That transfer-matrix
is the phase factor.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:16.720
It's written.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.670
You have a general
form for each layer.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:21.490
And we have many layers.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:24.730
It's just a multiplication
of those layers.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:28.600
So that's actually
most common to use.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:32.530
And people used
to see very often.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:36.550
And in fact, thin film
deposition is so common,

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:39.430
anybody of you can
pick up this methods

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.430
and do design of
multilayer films

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.650
to get desirable properties.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:47.600
And so that's, in fact--

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.640
I tell you that I never really,
seriously took a [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:52.420
wave class.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.280
That's how I-- where I started.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:55.120
I read the book.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.270
And you have to
have that patience

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:01.150
to write down all the terms.

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:02.800
And do your [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.750
And get the appreciation
for what's happening.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:10.040
It's a exercise.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:14.690
And if you take a square of
this reflection coefficient--

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:18.820
and here, I'm assuming
that it's a real, n1, n2,

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:22.180
n3 is real, because
say, clearly,

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:27.160
if it's a complex number,
r12 is a Fresnel coefficient

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.750
for each interface
you can calculate.

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:31.540
So even if it's
a complex number,

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:34.480
you can use all the
complex number operation,

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:35.980
do the square.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:39.400
But here, because I'm taking
a real so I can actually

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.490
write it, just in terms
of the regular square root

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:45.070
of the complex conjugate.

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:50.260
And you can see, now,
what's interesting,

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:54.190
what we want to get
in is the phenomena.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:58.630
And in my expression
for the reflectance,

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:03.310
I have a cosine 2 phi
term in both the numerator

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.270
and the denominator.

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:07.870
So you know, then,
this reflectance

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:11.500
was-- will be a periodic
variant function, because

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:13.520
of this cosine term.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:18.150
So let's use this
for discussion.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:26.600
And you can, of course,
use a single layer.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:31.700
And you take a dR, d phi, right?

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:36.800
This phi 2, you can say,
is really d cosine theta.

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.930
It's more like a thickness,
the effective thickness

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:41.210
for the direction
of propagation.

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:46.340
And divide by lambda 0,
so normalized to lamda 0.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:50.960
And this 2 phi 2 is because
of the round trait right?

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:55.340
If you look at the physics we
have, the reflection is coming,

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:56.180
reflect back.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:58.940
So it's a forward, backward.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:02.240
That's a factor of
2 in phase, right?

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:09.380
So if I take this into 0 and
you can find where the maximum,

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:17.550
minimum is, and it happens
when sine 2 phi 2 equals 0.

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:22.660
And for this, we have
different solutions,

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:24.910
whether it's a
maximum or minimum.

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:29.040
So this, of course,
happens when--

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.370
if I substitute
the phi 2 is 4 pi--

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.440
because that's a factor of 2--

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:41.910
n2, d2, cosine theta 2,
lambda 0 is a vacuum,

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:45.810
equals, say, multiple lamda pi.

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:47.730
That's when sine goes to 0.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:50.670
M is an integer, right?

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:53.530
But whether it's a
maximum or minimum--

00:06:53.530 --> 00:07:01.650
so when m is a odd number
so m equals 2l plus 1

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:03.240
and say odd number.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:08.160
And in this case, I list
that when theta equals 0,

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:13.950
you will find that
reflect test is

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:24.720
m1 n3, mass n2 square and m1,
n3, plus n2 square, square.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:28.020
So that's an interesting
expression, right?

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:32.880
Emittance, if I have those
conditions met at [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:33.870
incidence here--

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:36.900
there are also other
angle where you

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:38.670
can write the maximum, minimum.

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:42.610
I'm here writing this, because
this expression is simple.

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:46.150
You can see that, if
I choose-- if I have--

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:49.630
how I do antireflection
coating, right?

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:51.770
I have a surface.

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:55.270
And I don't want the
reflecting-- any reflection.

00:07:55.270 --> 00:08:00.250
And then, in this case, I
add a layer in between n2.

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:06.200
And you can say, well, n2 is
the square root of m1 and 3.

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:09.260
You've got a 0
reflection, right?

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:12.740
So by using the
interference phenomena,

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:16.460
this is a-- the periodic
variation of reflectance

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:19.220
is the interference phenomena.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:23.990
Interference of light, you
can have maximum reflection,

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:29.750
a minimum of reflection, right?

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:41.659
And when m equals 2l, in
this case, what happens is,

00:08:41.659 --> 00:08:43.480
again, I say it equals 0.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:54.005
You'll say your reflectance is
m1 minus n3, m1 plus n3 square.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.130
What does it tell you?

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:04.380
It tells you, even though
you put the layer there,

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:06.040
it doesn't matter.

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:10.390
It's just like an interference
between 1 and 3, right?

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:15.940
So you use this effects to do
different optical coatings.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:20.740
And here, of course, we're
only talking about one layer.

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:26.770
And you can coat many layers
to have desirable properties.

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:30.700
And people do that
for many applications.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:33.650
I'll mention more as we go on.

00:09:33.650 --> 00:09:37.390
So this is just a
simple, say, example.

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:40.360
One important characteristic
we'll learn from this

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:42.520
is when we consider a wave.

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:45.400
And here is phi 2, right?

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:48.220
If you look at
reflectance and what

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:53.200
you have is, say, a periodical
variation of reflectance,

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:56.440
that's the interference
phenomena, right?

00:09:56.440 --> 00:10:01.080
If you go to clean room, you
deposit a layer of film--

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:03.910
very often, some of you may
go in the clean room, deposit

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:05.430
a layer of film.

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:10.540
And experienced person will
look at the color of it,

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:12.280
because of the light,
white light comes

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:15.290
in is a broadband white light.

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:17.200
And because of the
interference effect,

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:21.710
that some color is reflecting
more, some color reflect less.

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:24.430
And if you do a lot of
this, you get experience.

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:26.620
You can tell from the
color, what's the thickness

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:29.990
range you have, OK?

00:10:29.990 --> 00:10:34.200
And, of course, the same film,
if you look at different angle,

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:36.510
you see different color.

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:39.980
And again, that's because,
if you look at phi 2,

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:41.810
it's angle dependent.

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:44.630
The phase of the light is, say--

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:49.230
it depends on the trajectory
it propagates in the material.

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.430
So this is a one layer film.

00:10:52.430 --> 00:11:04.580
And then we will go
multilayer, right?

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:07.890
It's hard to predict when
you add many, many layers

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:12.100
so the interference phenomenon
becomes very complicated.

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:17.050
And by, say, one special
multilayer is this--

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:17.980
yes?

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:20.890
AUDIENCE: Sorry, which values
of m correspond to minimum

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:24.100
and which one to maximum?

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:28.630
GANG CHEN: Which value of
M, so here, you can say,

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:33.568
when this we have
here it is the--

00:11:33.568 --> 00:11:37.680
this one is the minimum, right?

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:44.290
And this one, I think,
is the maximum, OK?

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:48.940
So when I have
multilayer of the film,

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:52.450
one special case is the
quarter of wavelengths.

00:11:52.450 --> 00:12:01.960
So when the tiers of layers
is, say the path lengths equals

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:06.820
quarter of wavelengths-- so what
I have, when I have multilayer,

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:10.660
you can choose a
layer one, layer two.

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:14.680
And you're repeating, layer
one, layer two, right?

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:19.510
And, in this case,
when I have 4 pi,

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:26.050
m1, d1, cosine theta
1, over lambda 0,

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:37.510
plus 4pi n2, d2, cosine
theta 2, over lambda 0.

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:44.390
And this is the effective
layer one and layer two, right?

00:12:44.390 --> 00:12:51.800
When they are effectively
equals 2l pi, I have--

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:59.240
in this case, the reflectance
actually reach a maximum.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:05.674
So what happens is,
if you look at your--

00:13:05.674 --> 00:13:09.040
as a function of for,
you can combine these two

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:13.600
as your effective phase,
let's say, effective phase.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:17.710
Or you say you can change
either wavelengths of light.

00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:21.820
If you make your structure
ready for different wavelengths

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:29.500
and would you see reflectance
have the almost perfect

00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:39.040
reflection region, OK?

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:41.260
And this is a 1.

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:44.580
This is a 0.

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:47.490
And when it's a
perfect reflection,

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:54.760
that means the light comes
in 100% reflected, right?

00:13:54.760 --> 00:14:01.630
And what emits
light is not allowed

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:07.720
in this periodic layer at this
angle of incidence, right?

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:10.780
And so, in optics, this
is called the stopband.

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:20.900
And in solid states, for
electrons and photons,

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:23.880
we call it the Band gap.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:29.580
If you recall when we discussed
the three dimensional crystals,

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:31.800
the semiconductor
band gap, that's

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:34.470
when the wave
functions of electrons

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:40.540
are not allowed in
this range of energy.

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:46.330
So you can say, I mentioned
before, for optics, people,

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:51.130
the research direction
is going from 1D to 3D.

00:14:51.130 --> 00:14:55.360
These object coatings, people
have developed over 100 years.

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:56.830
You'd rather have [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:56.830 --> 00:14:58.450
That's only 86.

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:02.800
He said, OK, let's do
3D analogy to crystals,

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:06.920
so that these stopbands
happens in all directions,

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:09.250
so that you can put
a real band gap.

00:15:09.250 --> 00:15:13.090
When you have such a crystal, no
matter which direction of light

00:15:13.090 --> 00:15:17.390
you shine onto it, it's
all reflecting, right?

00:15:17.390 --> 00:15:19.070
So that's why I joked before.

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:20.930
I had a colleague
before was saying,

00:15:20.930 --> 00:15:22.520
this is not photonic reflector.

00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:25.190
It's a Bragg reflector,
because this quarter

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:29.430
of wavelength stack is
called a Bragg reflector.

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:32.420
And this is the Bragg condition.

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:34.670
We talked before,
X-ray Bragg condition,

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:37.910
when you have pure [INAUDIBLE]
structure, the wave consoles,

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:38.420
right?

00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:41.270
So this is the [INAUDIBLE]
in Bragg condition.

00:15:41.270 --> 00:15:43.190
And this is a Bragg reflector.

00:15:51.290 --> 00:15:55.610
So it turns out, if you have
such a periodic structure,

00:15:55.610 --> 00:15:59.930
no matter how small, it's
refractory in that contrast.

00:15:59.930 --> 00:16:02.960
When I say, no matter, as long
as you have refractive index

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:04.800
contrast, right?

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:07.740
It can't be just no
contrast at all, right?

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:13.210
You can build many layers
and have such a phenomenon.

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:18.360
So normally, sometimes
when people make, say,

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:21.510
we want high reflection and
they don't have the material

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:25.260
that they get y interface,
y interface reflectance

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:28.020
isn't here, right?

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:31.600
You're limited by the
refractive index contrast.

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:37.320
So what you do is you use
multilayer Bragg reflectors

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:39.900
and make a multilayer structure.

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:44.760
And there are many,
many examples people

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:48.670
using those kind of effects.

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:52.110
One is a semiconductor laser.

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:55.840
So some of the
semiconductor lasers

00:16:55.840 --> 00:17:02.050
are actually made out for
gallium arsenide, aluminum

00:17:02.050 --> 00:17:04.470
arsenide, right?

00:17:04.470 --> 00:17:08.819
So while this-- normally,
a semiconductor laser

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:10.230
has two configuration.

00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:13.050
One is the [INAUDIBLE] meeting.

00:17:13.050 --> 00:17:15.240
So this is a quantum well.

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:17.460
We mentioned this
configuration before.

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:22.140
Quantum well, typically, very
thin, [INAUDIBLE],, so the light

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:24.560
will go much wider region.

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:27.030
So you use a total
internal reflection.

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:30.030
You put the aluminum
gallium arsenide

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:34.640
in the surrounding, which
has a lower refractive index.

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:38.560
And in that case, the
light is in this region.

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:39.490
And they propagate.

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:43.860
They come out from the
edge of the crystal.

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:45.780
And the light needs
to be amplified.

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:48.210
So it needs to go
a certain distance,

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:50.340
because the laser is an
amplified light emission,

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:51.390
right?

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:54.510
So the amplification is
a positive absorption

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:55.530
coefficient.

00:17:55.530 --> 00:17:57.780
We had, before,
absorption coefficient.

00:17:57.780 --> 00:18:00.250
The light is decaying
exponentially.

00:18:00.250 --> 00:18:04.030
And if you amplify the
growing exponentially--

00:18:04.030 --> 00:18:06.600
but you need a certain
distance to grow.

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:10.710
So in this [INAUDIBLE] laser,
the distance for the light

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:11.850
will oscillate.

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:14.610
It's typically a
few hundred microns.

00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:17.220
And so, it has the--
because of the-- what

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:20.560
they do is, after they
grow, the same device,

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:24.270
they will cleave it,
naval artillery interface,

00:18:24.270 --> 00:18:28.630
between air and
the semiconductor.

00:18:28.630 --> 00:18:30.870
So the light comes to the end.

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:32.520
They will be reflected.

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:35.100
And that's the
reflective, right?

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:38.190
If a reflectance-- if the
refractive index number tiered,

00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:41.360
typically, it's 3 to 4
gallium arsenide, right?

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:44.900
You can go to say that
reflectance is about, what?

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:49.690
If it's 3, that's
one quarter a square.

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:50.920
It's not big, right?

00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:58.130
So they need a long
distance to amplify.

00:18:58.130 --> 00:19:00.320
But people don't like
this configuration,

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:03.680
because they have to cleave with
a light to come from the edge.

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:06.470
And people spend a lot of
time trying to grow this,

00:19:06.470 --> 00:19:10.640
so that light can come
perpendicular to the surface.

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:15.130
In that case, this is
the quantum well region.

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:19.090
And they have to make
this certain thickness,

00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:23.630
half wavelengths or
full wavelengths, OK?

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:26.100
So that's a cavity.

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:30.450
But the light only travel a
very short distance, right?

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:33.690
So in this case, you
need multiple bounce.

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:36.690
You need a high reflectivity,
so that the light is bouncing

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:39.660
back and forth gets amplified.

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:42.210
And together, high
reflectivity they

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:44.430
will deposit this
Bragg reflector

00:19:44.430 --> 00:19:47.670
on the top and bottom, middle
gallium arsenide, aluminum

00:19:47.670 --> 00:19:49.680
arsenide.

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:54.120
So in that case, after,
say, the reflect inside

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:57.530
the surface can get over 99%.

00:19:57.530 --> 00:20:00.020
And the light comes
to some of the light

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:03.750
coming out perpendicular
to the substrate.

00:20:03.750 --> 00:20:07.250
So this is a VCSEL, Vertical
Cavity Surface Emitting

00:20:07.250 --> 00:20:09.760
Laser, ok?

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:13.180
So in fact, this is
what got me interested

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:15.460
in this multilayer
structure when

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:16.870
I was first doing research.

00:20:16.870 --> 00:20:19.960
I was aware that heat cannot
get out, because, in fact,

00:20:19.960 --> 00:20:24.130
those weeks when they were
conceived in the 1970s,

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:28.330
it was only until 1990s they
start to be able to sell it,

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:30.010
because of heating issue there.

00:20:30.010 --> 00:20:32.590
And when-- first,
when light goes

00:20:32.590 --> 00:20:34.430
through those
multilayer structures,

00:20:34.430 --> 00:20:39.100
they have to put a charge
going through that structure.

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:43.160
And this becomes very
electrically resistive.

00:20:43.160 --> 00:20:46.490
And it turns out,
also, I started

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:48.650
realizing that the
heat also has trouble

00:20:48.650 --> 00:20:51.300
to go through those
multilayer structures.

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:56.830
So that's why I worked on
it when I was doing my PhD.

00:20:56.830 --> 00:21:00.880
So that's the Bragg
reflector, right?

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:03.410
And if you use--

00:21:03.410 --> 00:21:05.410
for example, in your
lab, sometimes you

00:21:05.410 --> 00:21:08.350
buy an interference filter.

00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:10.640
And interference
filter is typically

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:13.460
also made of Bragg reflectors.

00:21:13.460 --> 00:21:18.110
And sometimes a
laser wavelength,

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:20.300
like you want to distinguish
different wavelengths,

00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:23.900
they all made of
Bragg reflectors.

00:21:23.900 --> 00:21:28.040
Another example, interesting
example of this multilayer, not

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:32.060
necessarily periodic structure,
is the selective surface.

00:21:44.900 --> 00:21:47.750
And when I say
selective surfac e,

00:21:47.750 --> 00:21:50.540
I mean wavelength
selective service.

00:21:50.540 --> 00:21:57.500
And one example is in the
solar thermal collectors.

00:21:57.500 --> 00:22:01.100
You want the surface
absorb some radiation.

00:22:01.100 --> 00:22:06.320
But you do not want the surface
to radiate heat out, right?

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:12.020
So they-- so ideally,
what you want--

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:17.780
because the solar radiation is
limited to about 2.4 microns.

00:22:17.780 --> 00:22:20.120
So wavelengths of
solar radiation

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:23.540
is typically less
than 2.4 microns.

00:22:23.540 --> 00:22:29.470
And you want this, ideally,
absorb emissivity course of 1

00:22:29.470 --> 00:22:31.990
for this wavelength.

00:22:31.990 --> 00:22:35.760
But emissivity
equals 0 beyond that.

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:40.710
When you heat up an object, you
think about Winslow lamda t,

00:22:40.710 --> 00:22:42.270
about 3,000.

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:47.670
So if your object is 500 Kelvin,
your wavelengths is 10 micron.

00:22:47.670 --> 00:22:49.110
That's the peak wavelength.

00:22:49.110 --> 00:22:54.470
So the [INAUDIBLE] body emission
from the surface is here.

00:22:54.470 --> 00:22:58.180
So if you can minimize
the emissivities to 0,

00:22:58.180 --> 00:23:01.720
you don't lose this
much here, right?

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:03.910
So that's what the
selective surface--

00:23:03.910 --> 00:23:08.880
ideally, I want the
emissivity 1 and 0 here

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:12.280
and how you make it, right?

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:14.170
You look at, say--

00:23:14.170 --> 00:23:18.160
typically, what is a surface
that's a good reflector?

00:23:18.160 --> 00:23:22.300
That means the-- its
remittance is going to be low.

00:23:22.300 --> 00:23:26.740
Typically, metal is a
good reflector, right?

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:31.750
And if you take a copper, you
take aluminum, they're shiny.

00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:34.090
If they're shiny, that's--

00:23:34.090 --> 00:23:36.280
again, you can calculate now.

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:37.930
Using the Fresnel
formula, you'll

00:23:37.930 --> 00:23:40.780
get a refractive index
or optical constants.

00:23:40.780 --> 00:23:41.980
You can calculate it.

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:45.950
But the emittance of
metal is typically low.

00:23:45.950 --> 00:23:49.700
The problem is, absorptions
is also low, right?

00:23:49.700 --> 00:23:54.400
So what people do is they
typically take this metal.

00:23:54.400 --> 00:23:59.640
And they will coat a few layers.

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:01.200
And this, you have to optimize.

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:03.310
Computer code, you
optimize different

00:24:03.310 --> 00:24:05.100
of the refractive index.

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:07.590
And [INAUDIBLE] this way,
the coat is aluminum.

00:24:07.590 --> 00:24:10.290
They start with
aluminum substrate.

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:13.890
And they coat--
they change the--

00:24:13.890 --> 00:24:16.350
I think, it's the nitrogen
environment or oxygen

00:24:16.350 --> 00:24:17.730
environment, right?

00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:19.660
They change of the
oxygen pressure.

00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:20.700
So now, it's together.

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:23.590
You get the aluminum
oxide, aluminum mixture.

00:24:23.590 --> 00:24:26.730
And if you change that
pressure, you'll--

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:30.180
each layer, you can actually
have a different mixture.

00:24:30.180 --> 00:24:35.380
And that way, they can
do it really cheap.

00:24:35.380 --> 00:24:37.705
And the performances
are pretty good.

00:24:37.705 --> 00:24:40.930
They can gather
emissivity, typically,

00:24:40.930 --> 00:24:44.170
around the 95% in this--

00:24:44.170 --> 00:24:49.180
the solar range and the
emittance in the infrared range

00:24:49.180 --> 00:24:54.770
as low as 5%, with three or
four layer coating, right?

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:58.880
I went to those companies
that do this kind of coating.

00:24:58.880 --> 00:25:03.620
And their vacuum chamber
is as a football field.

00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:07.310
And they're doing this, because
in China, this is, actually,

00:25:07.310 --> 00:25:10.470
a really low, widely
used solar thermal

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:13.880
in many, many households,
over 100 million square meters

00:25:13.880 --> 00:25:15.060
deployed, right?

00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:17.520
They do it really cheap, right?

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:21.090
So you have to look
at those coatings.

00:25:21.090 --> 00:25:23.140
And of course, when
you think of energy,

00:25:23.140 --> 00:25:24.660
you have to look at your cost.

00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:29.830
But what I want to tell
you is, now, you can do it.

00:25:29.830 --> 00:25:33.850
Using that transfer matrix,
it's a computer optimization.

00:25:33.850 --> 00:25:38.710
It's the one material you
choose that's realistic, right?

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:40.390
And if you design
that-- there are

00:25:40.390 --> 00:25:42.490
a lot of coating
companies outside that

00:25:42.490 --> 00:25:47.722
will be happy to do it for
you, if you have the money, OK?

00:25:50.240 --> 00:25:56.000
And let's-- so this is
a-- we have, really,

00:25:56.000 --> 00:26:01.550
the optical single layer
on multilayer calculation.

00:26:01.550 --> 00:26:03.200
We can do it now.

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:07.760
And to continue the
discussion, what I want

00:26:07.760 --> 00:26:14.660
is to look at the case
when the angle of incidence

00:26:14.660 --> 00:26:21.350
at the first layer is larger
than the critical angle

00:26:21.350 --> 00:26:25.330
of incidence, right?

00:26:25.330 --> 00:26:29.950
So in this case, at
a single interface,

00:26:29.950 --> 00:26:34.360
we know there's a total
internal reflection, right?

00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:38.790
But all the formula we learned--

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:43.560
for example, here, this
formula is still valid.

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:45.860
The only thing I
assume here is real--

00:26:45.860 --> 00:26:51.610
but say, if it's a complex,
you can use complex number.

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:53.950
And if it's angle larger
than angle of incidence,

00:26:53.950 --> 00:26:57.670
you can still use that formula.

00:26:57.670 --> 00:27:00.770
The math part is identical.

00:27:00.770 --> 00:27:04.720
So what's interesting is,
when the angle of incidence

00:27:04.720 --> 00:27:09.700
is larger than critical angle,
what I have is, cosine theta 2.

00:27:09.700 --> 00:27:16.400
You can say, cosine, theta 2
is 1 minus sine theta 2 square,

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:16.900
right?

00:27:16.900 --> 00:27:21.550
Sine theta 2, using
Snell law, is one minus--

00:27:21.550 --> 00:27:24.880
OK, let's say, sine
theta 2 square.

00:27:24.880 --> 00:27:34.610
And theta 2, sine theta 2
Snell law is sine theta 2, n2

00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:40.410
equals m1, sine theta 1, right?

00:27:40.410 --> 00:27:43.830
So you can see, in
this case, I have

00:27:43.830 --> 00:27:48.940
sine theta 2 is larger than
1 above the critical angle.

00:27:48.940 --> 00:27:50.610
So when I do my
cosine [INAUDIBLE],,

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:54.520
1 minus sine theta 2 square,
I get the imaginary number.

00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:56.080
And that's what we
discussed before.

00:27:56.080 --> 00:28:00.060
We have even less than
wave on the other side.

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:02.030
So if I have one
single interface,

00:28:02.030 --> 00:28:06.530
this everlasting wave decay
exponentially and doesn't

00:28:06.530 --> 00:28:09.100
carry energy to the other side.

00:28:09.100 --> 00:28:18.670
But-- so if I do this, what I
write is m1 sine, theta 1, n2 ,

00:28:18.670 --> 00:28:20.260
square minus 1.

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:23.680
I put that say, this now
becomes a real number.

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:33.040
And you can see, my cosine
theta 2 is an imaginary number.

00:28:33.040 --> 00:28:34.660
If I substitute into--

00:28:34.660 --> 00:28:40.860
this imaginary number
into here, now you

00:28:40.860 --> 00:28:45.620
will see my reflectance
is no longer 1.

00:28:45.620 --> 00:28:47.400
It could be long.

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:51.750
Say, it could be
less than 1, right?

00:28:51.750 --> 00:28:57.900
And similarly, your
transmittance is no longer 0.

00:28:57.900 --> 00:29:01.230
So this is what happens.

00:29:01.230 --> 00:29:13.530
The optical tunneling-- so this
is the tunneling Phenomenon.

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:18.070
So what happens, physically,
is, I have a high index

00:29:18.070 --> 00:29:20.610
material, m1, right?

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:26.260
And you shoot the light
above the critical angle.

00:29:26.260 --> 00:29:31.260
And normally, it's
totally reflected.

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:33.990
But on this side, there
is exponential decay

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:36.550
everlasting wave.

00:29:36.550 --> 00:29:42.360
And now, if-- here, if you see
ib, cosine theta is i times b,

00:29:42.360 --> 00:29:47.370
you can say, of course, that
depends on b times d2, right?

00:29:47.370 --> 00:29:50.130
Otherwise, that's
exponential decay.

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:51.690
It's an exponential function.

00:29:51.690 --> 00:29:57.600
And if this is really small,
that reflectance is what?

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:02.670
If this exponential term really
small, all this drop out,

00:30:02.670 --> 00:30:04.700
that's still what?

00:30:04.700 --> 00:30:14.420
And when the-- say, this
one, phi 2, d2 times that b,

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:18.770
divided by lambda,
it's not that, say--

00:30:18.770 --> 00:30:19.880
it's very small, actually.

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:22.100
Very-- it's very
large, it dropped to 0.

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:26.510
But when it's very small,
I can actually, now,

00:30:26.510 --> 00:30:32.555
have the reflectance
that's less than the--

00:30:35.210 --> 00:30:37.130
say, it's no longer 1.

00:30:37.130 --> 00:30:38.510
So that's the tunneling.

00:30:38.510 --> 00:30:41.180
So what happens is,
this everlasting wave,

00:30:41.180 --> 00:30:44.150
when I bring a [INAUDIBLE]
surface close by--

00:30:44.150 --> 00:30:45.500
so this is n2.

00:30:45.500 --> 00:30:47.270
This n2 could be vacuum.

00:30:47.270 --> 00:30:49.050
For example, I put a glass.

00:30:49.050 --> 00:30:50.510
I put the vacuum.

00:30:50.510 --> 00:30:53.270
I put another layer
of glass, right?

00:30:53.270 --> 00:30:57.230
And this, when I bring it close,
this wave start propagating

00:30:57.230 --> 00:31:00.020
again, it's allowable
because of the angle--

00:31:00.020 --> 00:31:03.510
Schnell law in the third medium.

00:31:03.510 --> 00:31:09.090
The angle, if I use the Schnell
law, is ly sine theta 1.

00:31:09.090 --> 00:31:12.300
n2 sine theta 2 already
says about critical angle.

00:31:12.300 --> 00:31:17.040
But the n3 sine theta 3.

00:31:17.040 --> 00:31:21.790
And in this medium, it
could be propagating, right?

00:31:21.790 --> 00:31:28.270
So in this case, what I'll
I have is R not equal to 1.

00:31:28.270 --> 00:31:31.480
And transmittance
is not equal to 0.

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:36.010
So I have final
transmission of tunneling

00:31:36.010 --> 00:31:37.960
of this evanescent wave.

00:31:37.960 --> 00:31:52.670
So tunneling of-- and if you
look at-- the distance for that

00:31:52.670 --> 00:31:59.420
to happen is when the separation
or the thickness of the film

00:31:59.420 --> 00:32:02.270
is comparable to wavelengths.

00:32:02.270 --> 00:32:04.940
That's an exponential
function, all right?

00:32:04.940 --> 00:32:09.000
So that gives you the
order of magnitude.

00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:14.360
So this tunneling phenomena
is valid for all waves, right?

00:32:14.360 --> 00:32:19.110
All those interference
phenomena, tunneling phenomena,

00:32:19.110 --> 00:32:20.790
Bragg reflectors.

00:32:20.790 --> 00:32:24.600
Bragg reflector-- in
the case of electron,

00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:26.040
it's the superlattice.

00:32:26.040 --> 00:32:28.980
You read [INAUDIBLE] paper.

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:33.330
That's a superlattice for
going from 3D to 1D, right?

00:32:33.330 --> 00:32:37.590
So all these are
applicable for as long

00:32:37.590 --> 00:32:39.790
as you treat them as waves.

00:32:39.790 --> 00:32:43.140
So just whether it's a
scalar wave for electron

00:32:43.140 --> 00:32:46.770
and a transverse wave for
vector wave for [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:46.770 --> 00:32:50.160
And the acoustic wave is
more complicated, right?

00:32:50.160 --> 00:32:52.710
Longitudinal and transverse.

00:32:52.710 --> 00:32:58.080
So if this is tunneling of
evanescent wave for photon,

00:32:58.080 --> 00:33:01.530
there is corresponding--
you can do all the analysis

00:33:01.530 --> 00:33:04.890
as we did for electron.

00:33:04.890 --> 00:33:12.000
So electron-- what we had before
is, at one single interface

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:16.020
when wave comes in,
you have reflection.

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:20.260
You have transmission, right?

00:33:20.260 --> 00:33:24.370
And if the energy is less
than the barrier height,

00:33:24.370 --> 00:33:27.760
you have total reflection, OK?

00:33:27.760 --> 00:33:32.400
But you have an evanescent
wave function on this side.

00:33:32.400 --> 00:33:35.600
And so again, if I
put a very thin film--

00:33:38.550 --> 00:33:40.680
and so this is my structure.

00:33:40.680 --> 00:33:47.310
Sandwich structure-- layer
1, layer 2, layer 3, right?

00:33:47.310 --> 00:33:51.150
So electron wave
launched from layer 1.

00:33:51.150 --> 00:33:54.480
And if the energy is less
than the barrier height

00:33:54.480 --> 00:33:59.560
U0, if this is the
energy of the electron--

00:33:59.560 --> 00:34:03.030
so in this case, I have
an evanescent wave.

00:34:03.030 --> 00:34:06.750
But if my layer
2 is thin enough,

00:34:06.750 --> 00:34:10.170
you can come out and
become propagating

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:13.010
into the third medium.

00:34:13.010 --> 00:34:17.070
So that's determining
of electron.

00:34:17.070 --> 00:34:20.340
And you could use, again,
the same analysis--

00:34:20.340 --> 00:34:24.929
similar analysis-- we
do for the optical wave.

00:34:24.929 --> 00:34:26.130
And this is a scalar wave.

00:34:26.130 --> 00:34:27.389
It's easier.

00:34:27.389 --> 00:34:30.719
You'll get the transmittance--
for electrons in this case,

00:34:30.719 --> 00:34:45.880
it's a 4E energy U0 minus
E and 4E U0 minus E plus U0

00:34:45.880 --> 00:35:03.230
squared sinh function square
D and 2m U0 minus E and h bar

00:35:03.230 --> 00:35:04.620
square.

00:35:04.620 --> 00:35:07.620
So this is the transmittance.

00:35:07.620 --> 00:35:12.010
And typically, the sinh function
is exponential function.

00:35:12.010 --> 00:35:13.390
So it grows large.

00:35:13.390 --> 00:35:16.170
So the first tau
term can be dropped.

00:35:16.170 --> 00:35:19.110
If you do that, if you
then, say, [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:19.110 --> 00:35:28.680
the exponential, this one
will be 16E U0 minus E over U0

00:35:28.680 --> 00:35:35.685
square exponential 2 K2 d.

00:35:35.685 --> 00:35:40.240
K2 is what we have
in this bracket.

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:43.420
And that's the wave vector
in the second medium.

00:35:46.730 --> 00:35:47.270
OK.

00:35:47.270 --> 00:35:51.830
So we gave the order of
magnitude of this before.

00:35:51.830 --> 00:35:54.050
But let's do it again.

00:35:54.050 --> 00:35:57.710
We said, for optical
waves, the tunneling

00:35:57.710 --> 00:36:00.920
happened when the thickness
is comparable to wavelength.

00:36:03.470 --> 00:36:10.180
So if I do thermal radiation, my
wavelength's around 10 micron.

00:36:10.180 --> 00:36:13.870
And that thickness
is 10 micron range.

00:36:13.870 --> 00:36:16.320
You start to have tunneling.

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:20.610
But for the electron
wave, this K2

00:36:20.610 --> 00:36:29.810
is 2m U0 minus E h bar square.

00:36:29.810 --> 00:36:31.510
That's magnitude.

00:36:31.510 --> 00:36:35.310
So if we go to estimate
again-- so here,

00:36:35.310 --> 00:36:40.410
the electron mass 9.1
10 to the minus 31.

00:36:40.410 --> 00:36:44.370
So if this U0 minus E is
about 1 electron volts

00:36:44.370 --> 00:36:50.781
order of magnitude, it's
1.6 10 to the minus 19.

00:36:50.781 --> 00:36:54.350
And h bar is 1 10
to the minus 34.

00:36:54.350 --> 00:36:56.510
So square 68.

00:36:56.510 --> 00:36:59.450
So this one, I
will get 6 then 10

00:36:59.450 --> 00:37:03.232
to the 9th inverse Armstrong--

00:37:03.232 --> 00:37:04.820
or no, inverse meter.

00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:16.600
So you can say, essentially,
when K times D--

00:37:16.600 --> 00:37:20.740
when D is of the order of 1
Armstrong 10 to the minus 10,

00:37:20.740 --> 00:37:26.810
my exponential is pretty
appreciable, right?

00:37:26.810 --> 00:37:29.480
So in the case of
electron tunneling,

00:37:29.480 --> 00:37:32.350
that happens in the
Armstrong range.

00:37:32.350 --> 00:37:36.520
In the case of photon
tunneling, happens

00:37:36.520 --> 00:37:42.370
of the order of, say,
optical wavelengths,

00:37:42.370 --> 00:37:44.770
say thermal wavelengths, right?

00:37:44.770 --> 00:37:49.040
[INAUDIBLE] the radiation peak
at 300 is about 10 microns.

00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:51.680
So it's a very different scale.

00:37:51.680 --> 00:37:55.650
But the physics is all similar.

00:37:55.650 --> 00:37:59.420
So there are really a
lot of interesting ideas

00:37:59.420 --> 00:38:01.880
using those phenomena.

00:38:01.880 --> 00:38:08.660
I told you before, this Binnig
when he interviewed at the IBM,

00:38:08.660 --> 00:38:09.230
right?

00:38:09.230 --> 00:38:13.540
So he was asked to
think about the idea

00:38:13.540 --> 00:38:17.260
to measure roughness of the
surface down to Armstrong,

00:38:17.260 --> 00:38:17.760
right?

00:38:17.760 --> 00:38:21.790
So he did this using this idea.

00:38:21.790 --> 00:38:24.690
So this is layer 1, a probe.

00:38:24.690 --> 00:38:27.540
Layer 2 is a vacuum.

00:38:27.540 --> 00:38:31.550
Layer 3 is the surface you're
going to measure, right?

00:38:31.550 --> 00:38:37.250
And you apply a bias voltage.

00:38:37.250 --> 00:38:39.360
And you scale over the surface.

00:38:39.360 --> 00:38:40.370
You measure current.

00:38:40.370 --> 00:38:42.470
You use your current
[INAUDIBLE] feedback.

00:38:42.470 --> 00:38:44.390
That's tunneling, right?

00:38:44.390 --> 00:38:46.670
So if your current--

00:38:46.670 --> 00:38:48.380
based on the current
and your feedback,

00:38:48.380 --> 00:38:53.020
you can maintain your
height, not crash your T.

00:38:53.020 --> 00:38:55.210
So of course, they say,
they did the experiment.

00:38:55.210 --> 00:38:56.590
It worked.

00:38:56.590 --> 00:38:58.600
But then, they
actually didn't know

00:38:58.600 --> 00:39:01.030
where the tunneling
happened, because this

00:39:01.030 --> 00:39:02.980
is atomic scale, right?

00:39:02.980 --> 00:39:06.710
If you look at the probe,
they will cut the metal

00:39:06.710 --> 00:39:10.053
and cut a sharp
tip or [INAUDIBLE]..

00:39:10.053 --> 00:39:12.470
So there are a lot of papers--
where this tunneling really

00:39:12.470 --> 00:39:13.040
happened.

00:39:13.040 --> 00:39:15.520
And you magnify the probe.

00:39:15.520 --> 00:39:18.710
Eventually, there is
one atom in the front.

00:39:18.710 --> 00:39:24.120
And that's probably where the
current goes through, right?

00:39:24.120 --> 00:39:29.250
And so Binnig got a
lower price for this.

00:39:29.250 --> 00:39:34.330
But they say, a lot of you use
atomic force microscope, right?

00:39:34.330 --> 00:39:37.830
That's also his invention.

00:39:37.830 --> 00:39:40.490
This was what he
did at IBM Zurich.

00:39:40.490 --> 00:39:45.620
And he went to Stanford
for sabbatical.

00:39:45.620 --> 00:39:49.970
But everybody was asking how
to measure insulators, right?

00:39:49.970 --> 00:39:53.030
This was a limit
to the conductors.

00:39:53.030 --> 00:39:55.390
So I read one of his articles.

00:39:55.390 --> 00:39:56.560
He said one day he was sick.

00:39:56.560 --> 00:40:00.470
He was lying in his apartment
and looked out the roof

00:40:00.470 --> 00:40:06.000
and came up with this idea
of atomic force microscope.

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:06.500
Why?

00:40:06.500 --> 00:40:09.650
Because there is
always fundamentally--

00:40:09.650 --> 00:40:13.890
he said the thinking is,
OK, you have a solid.

00:40:13.890 --> 00:40:16.840
And you have cantilever, right?

00:40:16.840 --> 00:40:20.460
And your first
question, he say, is,

00:40:20.460 --> 00:40:24.120
I'm going to scratch the
surface or not, right?

00:40:24.120 --> 00:40:27.300
If you use a cantilever
on the surface,

00:40:27.300 --> 00:40:29.370
you're worried about
that you scratch

00:40:29.370 --> 00:40:34.060
the surface, how you're going
to measure atomic roughness.

00:40:34.060 --> 00:40:37.360
But he now said, this is
where the basic knowledge--

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:40.130
this spring constant, right?

00:40:40.130 --> 00:40:41.840
So you know the vibration.

00:40:41.840 --> 00:40:44.600
You know the spring
constant between atoms.

00:40:44.600 --> 00:40:46.880
And you go to see--
you go to look at,

00:40:46.880 --> 00:40:49.490
what's the spring
constant of cantilever?

00:40:49.490 --> 00:40:52.900
What's the spring
constant between atoms?

00:40:52.900 --> 00:40:55.450
And the atom's spring
constant is much stiffer.

00:40:55.450 --> 00:40:57.700
That means the cantilever
will bend, right?

00:40:57.700 --> 00:41:01.230
The stiff one moves less.

00:41:01.230 --> 00:41:03.240
So people think of this idea.

00:41:03.240 --> 00:41:05.740
That's because we
have the knowledge.

00:41:05.740 --> 00:41:10.020
You have to apply your knowledge
and think about this-- this is,

00:41:10.020 --> 00:41:11.340
say, possible, right?

00:41:11.340 --> 00:41:13.360
So he has this.

00:41:13.360 --> 00:41:15.900
And of course,
there is deflection.

00:41:15.900 --> 00:41:17.550
So he knows how to
measure deflection,

00:41:17.550 --> 00:41:20.700
because he put a scanning
tunneling microscope here.

00:41:20.700 --> 00:41:23.520
That cantilever
itself is a conductor.

00:41:23.520 --> 00:41:26.580
So if you look at how the
AFM-- at the beginning,

00:41:26.580 --> 00:41:29.550
it was the AFM, atomic
force cantilever

00:41:29.550 --> 00:41:33.330
plus a scanning tunneling tip
to measure this deflection

00:41:33.330 --> 00:41:36.340
of the cantilever.

00:41:36.340 --> 00:41:40.910
Later on, people said, OK, there
is a much easier way to do it.

00:41:40.910 --> 00:41:43.300
And that's by
optical deflection.

00:41:43.300 --> 00:41:48.370
Because optical deflection is
magnifying the small change

00:41:48.370 --> 00:41:52.370
in the height into angle.

00:41:52.370 --> 00:41:55.720
Angle times this
distance is a magnifier,

00:41:55.720 --> 00:41:59.500
because that's more angle
if you magnify the distance.

00:41:59.500 --> 00:42:01.690
You put a position detector.

00:42:01.690 --> 00:42:05.210
And you measure this voltage
change at different positions.

00:42:05.210 --> 00:42:09.100
So that became much
easier to implement.

00:42:09.100 --> 00:42:10.780
So you've got to
have your knowledge,

00:42:10.780 --> 00:42:12.850
and you've got to be creative.

00:42:12.850 --> 00:42:18.760
Sometimes, you also have
to have a break, right?

00:42:18.760 --> 00:42:20.620
OK.

00:42:20.620 --> 00:42:23.550
That's electron
tunneling, right?

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:28.030
And the photon tunneling-- you
can easily think of the same.

00:42:28.030 --> 00:42:29.510
So there are people
who developed

00:42:29.510 --> 00:42:33.060
the different way of a
photon tunneling microscope.

00:42:33.060 --> 00:42:35.490
One way is-- this is a prism.

00:42:35.490 --> 00:42:38.350
You launch the light
perpendicular to the surface.

00:42:38.350 --> 00:42:40.770
So there is no total
internal reflection.

00:42:40.770 --> 00:42:44.260
But at this surface, there is
a total internal reflection.

00:42:44.260 --> 00:42:47.370
So here-- your reflected
light, your wave.

00:42:47.370 --> 00:42:50.460
It has an evanescent wave here.

00:42:50.460 --> 00:42:51.780
And OK.

00:42:51.780 --> 00:42:54.840
Now, you bring a surface.

00:42:54.840 --> 00:42:55.650
That's a vacuum.

00:42:55.650 --> 00:42:57.740
It's layer 2.

00:42:57.740 --> 00:43:01.270
You bring your third
layer, which is a probe.

00:43:01.270 --> 00:43:02.620
You brought it close.

00:43:02.620 --> 00:43:05.620
That will interact with
this evanescent wave

00:43:05.620 --> 00:43:10.020
and scatter them into
propagating wave.

00:43:10.020 --> 00:43:12.820
So that's ways people do--

00:43:12.820 --> 00:43:16.230
in fact, I think Professor
Peter Shor in the department,

00:43:16.230 --> 00:43:21.010
he will do this to try to
image, a say, nanoparticle

00:43:21.010 --> 00:43:23.790
or biological
object with under--

00:43:23.790 --> 00:43:26.250
say, try to be the
optical resolution,

00:43:26.250 --> 00:43:28.890
the wavelength limit, right?

00:43:28.890 --> 00:43:30.820
There are different
ways to do it.

00:43:30.820 --> 00:43:34.680
But sometimes, there are
also crazy ways to think.

00:43:34.680 --> 00:43:36.420
Let me give you an
idea that didn't work.

00:43:36.420 --> 00:43:38.790
I don't think it worked.

00:43:38.790 --> 00:43:44.090
And there was a big
project by DARPA

00:43:44.090 --> 00:43:49.190
trying to do the tunneling
energy conversion.

00:43:49.190 --> 00:43:54.530
And so basically, you
heat up a surface.

00:43:54.530 --> 00:43:55.940
This is T hot.

00:43:55.940 --> 00:43:58.830
This is T cold, right?

00:43:58.830 --> 00:44:01.020
And they want to bring
the two surfaces really

00:44:01.020 --> 00:44:04.000
close so that electron
from the hot side

00:44:04.000 --> 00:44:05.790
tunneled to the cold side.

00:44:05.790 --> 00:44:08.550
And you'd have power
generation, all right?

00:44:08.550 --> 00:44:09.390
There's a company.

00:44:09.390 --> 00:44:10.890
You can search the web.

00:44:10.890 --> 00:44:12.150
It's called Cool Chips.

00:44:12.150 --> 00:44:13.780
It's still there.

00:44:13.780 --> 00:44:15.340
And they want to
do cooling also.

00:44:15.340 --> 00:44:16.810
You apply current.

00:44:16.810 --> 00:44:20.430
And you go tunnel electron
from one side to the other.

00:44:20.430 --> 00:44:23.340
Principle-wise, it's all fine.

00:44:23.340 --> 00:44:26.720
But doing a thermal
system with two fields

00:44:26.720 --> 00:44:30.160
of Armstrong-level separation,
you screwed most of the time,

00:44:30.160 --> 00:44:30.660
right?

00:44:30.660 --> 00:44:34.530
The contact kills you, right?

00:44:34.530 --> 00:44:37.540
And in fact, I say
there is also--

00:44:37.540 --> 00:44:39.480
even last week, I
was in a workshop.

00:44:39.480 --> 00:44:44.010
Some people were mentioning our
work, because they want to do--

00:44:44.010 --> 00:44:45.750
this was a Stanford group.

00:44:45.750 --> 00:44:48.840
They want to do the tunneling--

00:44:48.840 --> 00:44:51.840
the thermionic emission, right?

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:56.000
Thermionic emission is a
[INAUDIBLE] work function,

00:44:56.000 --> 00:44:56.500
right?

00:44:56.500 --> 00:44:58.230
The few electron volts--

00:44:58.230 --> 00:45:02.460
so if you heat up electron,
heat up the object,

00:45:02.460 --> 00:45:06.730
the electron will evaporate
physically out of the surface,

00:45:06.730 --> 00:45:07.230
right?

00:45:07.230 --> 00:45:10.080
That's thermionic
emission, right?

00:45:10.080 --> 00:45:15.180
So the problem is that when
you have two surfaces close-by,

00:45:15.180 --> 00:45:16.130
and--

00:45:16.130 --> 00:45:17.780
say not close-by, far apart.

00:45:17.780 --> 00:45:21.230
When electrons go out,
they create a potential.

00:45:21.230 --> 00:45:24.170
Because a charge in a
vacuum has the potential.

00:45:24.170 --> 00:45:27.270
The other electron now
will see that potential.

00:45:27.270 --> 00:45:28.800
It's hard to get
to the other side.

00:45:28.800 --> 00:45:30.320
So that's a space charge effect.

00:45:33.480 --> 00:45:35.337
So their theory-- and
this Stanford group

00:45:35.337 --> 00:45:36.170
was also doing this.

00:45:36.170 --> 00:45:39.110
They said, OK, we're doing MEMS
fabrication to make it smaller.

00:45:39.110 --> 00:45:41.940
So the reduced space charge
effect, which is fine.

00:45:41.940 --> 00:45:44.190
But then, when people say,
OK, why don't you do this?

00:45:44.190 --> 00:45:45.890
They say, oh, Gang
Chen has a study.

00:45:45.890 --> 00:45:51.160
Because when you're getting
really close, what happens is,

00:45:51.160 --> 00:45:55.810
the tunneling of light actually
increases heat transfer,

00:45:55.810 --> 00:45:59.170
which you don't want in
energy conversion, right?

00:45:59.170 --> 00:46:02.920
So you don't want to get
the T hot, T cold too close

00:46:02.920 --> 00:46:04.300
below micron.

00:46:04.300 --> 00:46:07.990
The radiation tunneling
starts to come in and increase

00:46:07.990 --> 00:46:11.710
your heat transfer, because
this vacuum is good.

00:46:11.710 --> 00:46:16.710
But say, so you have to see,
where is your balance point?

00:46:16.710 --> 00:46:19.170
And of course, we do
another experiment.

00:46:19.170 --> 00:46:22.200
We do another experiment
where this is T hot.

00:46:22.200 --> 00:46:24.370
This is T cold.

00:46:24.370 --> 00:46:27.580
And we are starting
the radiation transfer.

00:46:27.580 --> 00:46:30.820
That's when the two
surfaces get really close.

00:46:30.820 --> 00:46:32.780
I can have tunneling.

00:46:32.780 --> 00:46:35.350
And that would significantly
[INAUDIBLE] the black body

00:46:35.350 --> 00:46:38.560
radiation, OK?

00:46:38.560 --> 00:46:42.070
And so this is different.

00:46:42.070 --> 00:46:45.580
A lot of very fancy,
interesting physical phenomena

00:46:45.580 --> 00:46:46.930
happens at this scale.

00:46:46.930 --> 00:46:50.740
And also an interesting
question is asked.

00:46:50.740 --> 00:46:53.480
What are you going
to do with it, right?

00:46:53.480 --> 00:46:58.190
If you invent this,
it's a great impact.

00:46:58.190 --> 00:47:02.320
And some of those
ideas don't work.

00:47:02.320 --> 00:47:04.450
But it's good to know
what's the limit.

00:47:07.340 --> 00:47:11.390
OK, so what we have done--

00:47:11.390 --> 00:47:14.060
if you recall, when
we started chapter 5,

00:47:14.060 --> 00:47:17.540
this is the actual chapter I
spent the most time so far,

00:47:17.540 --> 00:47:18.680
right?

00:47:18.680 --> 00:47:27.810
And what we want to find
out is the transport

00:47:27.810 --> 00:47:29.300
between two points--

00:47:29.300 --> 00:47:32.650
point 1 and point 2.

00:47:32.650 --> 00:47:39.350
And if I know the
transmission tau from 1 to 2,

00:47:39.350 --> 00:47:44.900
and I know transmission
from 2 to 1,

00:47:44.900 --> 00:47:47.636
and then I know
how many, how much,

00:47:47.636 --> 00:47:51.410
how fast I can count to
see, what's the energy flux?

00:47:51.410 --> 00:47:52.640
What's momentum flux?

00:47:52.640 --> 00:47:55.130
What's the charge flux, right?

00:47:55.130 --> 00:47:59.510
You can do those type of
transport calculations.

00:47:59.510 --> 00:48:03.410
And what we have done
in the past few lectures

00:48:03.410 --> 00:48:06.350
is to see how we calculate
that transmission from one

00:48:06.350 --> 00:48:10.040
point to the other,
be it the interface,

00:48:10.040 --> 00:48:13.680
be it the many layers, right?

00:48:13.680 --> 00:48:15.510
So now, we can
use this knowledge

00:48:15.510 --> 00:48:20.190
to put the story together
and write down the transport

00:48:20.190 --> 00:48:21.660
formula.

00:48:21.660 --> 00:48:25.590
If you recall, so far
we have been doing--

00:48:25.590 --> 00:48:27.810
the best, in terms of
the energy, for example,

00:48:27.810 --> 00:48:30.450
is the heat capacity,
the energy stored

00:48:30.450 --> 00:48:33.690
per unit volume, which
is a density of states

00:48:33.690 --> 00:48:36.960
times the number of--

00:48:36.960 --> 00:48:38.670
say, particle average
lumber, particle

00:48:38.670 --> 00:48:41.740
per states times the
energy per state, right?

00:48:41.740 --> 00:48:43.950
So that's how they
integrate together,

00:48:43.950 --> 00:48:46.530
get the total energy
per unit volume.

00:48:46.530 --> 00:48:51.630
But now, we're looking at the
flux, and then from one point

00:48:51.630 --> 00:48:52.810
to the other.

00:48:52.810 --> 00:48:56.460
So let's look at this.

00:48:56.460 --> 00:49:07.650
And this is the
Landauer formalism.

00:49:11.800 --> 00:49:16.360
Landau formalism as the
actual view of transport

00:49:16.360 --> 00:49:17.450
is relatively new.

00:49:17.450 --> 00:49:23.740
It's much later than others,
like [INAUDIBLE] or [INAUDIBLE]

00:49:23.740 --> 00:49:25.840
response theory transport.

00:49:25.840 --> 00:49:32.990
This was developed in the 60s
