01:07:42.310 --> 01:07:43.435
This side is 2.

01:07:46.350 --> 01:07:51.350
And I can apply my
expression here.

01:07:51.350 --> 01:07:55.190
And to get the heat
flux, it's proportional

01:07:55.190 --> 01:07:59.410
to the temperature difference
of the two sides, right?

01:07:59.410 --> 01:08:02.350
So in this case, what I have--

01:08:02.350 --> 01:08:05.800
remember, if you look at
this, what I'm doing--

01:08:05.800 --> 01:08:08.560
this is the photon coming
towards the interface from 1

01:08:08.560 --> 01:08:10.210
to medium 2, right?

01:08:10.210 --> 01:08:15.740
So this is the corresponding
temperature of this E1.

01:08:15.740 --> 01:08:18.080
And I call this, actually,
immediate photon.

01:08:18.080 --> 01:08:20.830
This is the incoming photon.

01:08:20.830 --> 01:08:22.990
And it's a highly long
equilibrium process

01:08:22.990 --> 01:08:24.450
at the interface.

01:08:24.450 --> 01:08:27.760
Interface is always
non-equilibrium transport.

01:08:27.760 --> 01:08:31.620
There is no-- it's
actually hard to think.

01:08:31.620 --> 01:08:34.800
And it's not an
equilibrium path.

01:08:34.800 --> 01:08:38.430
And this other side,
I have E2 come in,

01:08:38.430 --> 01:08:42.920
reflection and transmission.

01:08:42.920 --> 01:08:45.240
So that's what happens.

01:08:45.240 --> 01:08:48.600
And based on the
expression I have,

01:08:48.600 --> 01:08:52.470
you can say here, if the
temperature difference

01:08:52.470 --> 01:08:54.390
on the two sides are
not very different,

01:08:54.390 --> 01:08:57.240
I can do a
differentiation of that f.

01:08:57.240 --> 01:09:09.580
So I can write this as df dT
times TE1 minus TE2, right?

01:09:09.580 --> 01:09:11.200
OK?

01:09:11.200 --> 01:09:17.439
And the other T over q is
defined as the thermal boundary

01:09:17.439 --> 01:09:19.420
resistance.

01:09:19.420 --> 01:09:24.939
So if I have a boundary,
I have reflection,

01:09:24.939 --> 01:09:27.270
I will get resistance.

01:09:27.270 --> 01:09:28.890
And it's the same
for any material.

01:09:28.890 --> 01:09:30.450
If you have charge
reflection, you

01:09:30.450 --> 01:09:32.361
get the electron resistance.

01:09:32.361 --> 01:09:33.944
You will have, say,
photon reflection,

01:09:33.944 --> 01:09:37.149
you get thermal resistance.

01:09:37.149 --> 01:09:48.460
And this q 1 2 equals TE1
minus TE2 and R prime.

01:09:48.460 --> 01:09:51.580
And let me actually
also put this.

01:09:51.580 --> 01:09:53.319
It's RE prime double prime.

01:09:53.319 --> 01:09:56.590
That's a specific thermal
boundary resistance.

01:09:56.590 --> 01:10:02.110
So the unit here will be
meters squared kelvin per watt

01:10:02.110 --> 01:10:02.860
for this.

01:10:06.940 --> 01:10:09.140
It's specific per unit area.

01:10:09.140 --> 01:10:15.750
So if you you know the area,
you divide it by that, OK?

01:10:15.750 --> 01:10:21.030
So here, based on
this, you can say,

01:10:21.030 --> 01:10:28.590
I can calculate the thermal
boundary resistance.

01:10:28.590 --> 01:10:45.980
E and 0 2 pi d phi 0 pi over 2,
and d theta V Z1 and tau 1 2 2,

01:10:45.980 --> 01:10:50.720
and E1 dfdtT.

01:10:50.720 --> 01:10:57.980
And then, substates
E1 4 pi and dE1.

01:10:57.980 --> 01:11:00.593
So in theory now, you
can calculate this term.

01:11:00.593 --> 01:11:01.760
You calculate the substates.

01:11:01.760 --> 01:11:03.140
You know this.

01:11:03.140 --> 01:11:04.655
You know the band structure.

01:11:04.655 --> 01:11:07.652
You know this.

01:11:07.652 --> 01:11:12.550
This velocity, I'll
