01:11:12.550 --> 01:11:15.840
So you can calculate
everything, right?

01:11:19.460 --> 01:11:21.470
But you should ask me a problem.

01:11:23.980 --> 01:11:25.780
I'm waiting for the
question to be asked.

01:11:28.447 --> 01:11:30.030
You see, there are
some problems here.

01:11:30.030 --> 01:11:34.890
That is, if my transmissivity
equals 1, what happens?

01:11:39.950 --> 01:11:45.740
If my transmissivity
equals 1, is this 0?

01:11:45.740 --> 01:11:48.028
Resistance is not 0, right?

01:11:48.028 --> 01:11:48.945
So what's the problem?

01:11:51.890 --> 01:11:58.360
In fact, the first paper on
this was written by [INAUDIBLE]..

01:11:58.360 --> 01:12:01.240
I think it was a
Stanford professor.

01:12:01.240 --> 01:12:03.400
And he had a discussion of this.

01:12:03.400 --> 01:12:04.480
Let's see.

01:12:04.480 --> 01:12:08.770
He said, what happens if you
have an imaginary surface,

01:12:08.770 --> 01:12:09.460
right?

01:12:09.460 --> 01:12:10.930
He said, this is not applicable.

01:12:10.930 --> 01:12:14.680
You have to do our
[INAUDIBLE] free path.

01:12:18.640 --> 01:12:21.010
But then, too,
the problem really

01:12:21.010 --> 01:12:23.840
lies in how we
define temperature.

01:12:23.840 --> 01:12:26.940
This is a highly long
equilibrium process

01:12:26.940 --> 01:12:29.280
at the interface, right?

01:12:29.280 --> 01:12:36.710
And temperature is defined based
on more equilibrium concept.

01:12:36.710 --> 01:12:41.630
And if we think
about at equilibrium,

01:12:41.630 --> 01:12:47.270
the distribution function
is isotropic, right?

01:12:47.270 --> 01:12:49.130
There's no direction.

01:12:51.930 --> 01:12:55.170
And what happens
at this interface--

01:12:55.170 --> 01:12:57.510
if you look at it, I say,
this is the incoming.

01:12:57.510 --> 01:12:59.940
This is the other side incoming.

01:12:59.940 --> 01:13:02.230
Each side, you have a
different spectrum, right?

01:13:02.230 --> 01:13:06.450
You know that this together
gives me the spectrum.

01:13:06.450 --> 01:13:09.790
Different energy
distribution, right?

01:13:09.790 --> 01:13:12.670
So what I really
say-- what's really

01:13:12.670 --> 01:13:15.430
the temperature at this point?

01:13:15.430 --> 01:13:16.740
The temperature at the point--

01:13:16.740 --> 01:13:21.490
if you think about the local
energy density at a point

01:13:21.490 --> 01:13:22.690
gives you the temperature.

01:13:22.690 --> 01:13:25.930
If you go back to the
specific heat calculation,

01:13:25.930 --> 01:13:30.410
the local energy is relative
to local temperature.

01:13:30.410 --> 01:13:36.690
And that local energy is
defined based on all directions.

01:13:36.690 --> 01:13:40.830
And when I look at this point,
my energy on the left side--

01:13:40.830 --> 01:13:42.260
let's say this is my T1.

01:13:42.260 --> 01:13:43.310
This is my T2.

01:13:43.310 --> 01:13:46.460
I say it's different
than T1, T2.

01:13:46.460 --> 01:13:50.450
That's because when I
look at the point 1,

01:13:50.450 --> 01:13:53.480
I have those 4 lines coming in.

01:13:53.480 --> 01:13:57.040
That has the
characteristic-- that's T1.

01:13:57.040 --> 01:14:02.237
That's only 180 degrees
on this side, right?

01:14:02.237 --> 01:14:03.070
That's the incoming.

01:14:05.910 --> 01:14:07.770
The other is outgoing.

01:14:07.770 --> 01:14:09.190
That's the other 180.

01:14:09.190 --> 01:14:10.602
And let's look at this.

01:14:10.602 --> 01:14:11.310
This is incoming.

01:14:11.310 --> 01:14:12.690
That's this side.

01:14:12.690 --> 01:14:15.720
The outgoing on this
side is a reflection

01:14:15.720 --> 01:14:18.220
of the surface itself.

01:14:18.220 --> 01:14:22.740
So reflection depends on
reflectance and the angle

01:14:22.740 --> 01:14:23.400
dependence.

01:14:23.400 --> 01:14:27.170
It may not be symmetric at all.

01:14:27.170 --> 01:14:29.090
That's one part.

01:14:29.090 --> 01:14:32.540
The other part is my
transmission here on this side.

01:14:32.540 --> 01:14:34.240
Once it transmitted,
that's again

01:14:34.240 --> 01:14:37.720
modulated by my transmission,
which has angle dependence.

01:14:37.720 --> 01:14:42.520
So this is the transmission.

01:14:42.520 --> 01:14:44.500
So I add these two together.

01:14:44.500 --> 01:14:48.310
That gives me-- they go
into other directions.

01:14:48.310 --> 01:14:49.450
That's that part.

01:14:49.450 --> 01:14:51.760
And then, it may
not completely--

01:14:51.760 --> 01:14:54.480
unsymmetric.

01:14:54.480 --> 01:14:56.990
And the local energy
should be defined based on,

01:14:56.990 --> 01:15:00.560
you add this part, add
this part, add this part.

01:15:00.560 --> 01:15:06.260
And divide by if it's the
equivalent temperature, right?

01:15:06.260 --> 01:15:09.680
So if you do equivalent
temperature, T!

01:15:09.680 --> 01:15:14.900
1 and T2, you redo your energy
balance, it will be different.

01:15:14.900 --> 01:15:17.540
Because this T1 clearly
depends on not only

01:15:17.540 --> 01:15:20.720
the temperature that comes
in, but also the transmittance

01:15:20.720 --> 01:15:24.630
from the other side, right?

01:15:24.630 --> 01:15:27.570
So you will have to
write the relation

01:15:27.570 --> 01:15:30.090
at the end of your expression.

01:15:30.090 --> 01:15:31.380
That's in the book.

01:15:31.380 --> 01:15:33.090
I'm not going to rewrite it.

01:15:33.090 --> 01:15:36.760
You get a different thermal
boundary resistance.

01:15:36.760 --> 01:15:43.760
And the limit when transmission
is 1 is just 0, OK?

01:15:43.760 --> 01:15:44.930
I wrote that.

01:15:44.930 --> 01:15:48.120
And people typically
don't know it.

01:15:48.120 --> 01:15:53.090
So I keep telling my own group,
you need to read my chapter 5.

01:15:53.090 --> 01:15:56.960
Because you have to read
carefully to understand that.

01:15:56.960 --> 01:16:00.440
Because very often, people
are comparing wrong things.

01:16:00.440 --> 01:16:03.800
They do an experiment.

01:16:03.800 --> 01:16:06.650
And they will compare
it with this theory.

01:16:06.650 --> 01:16:09.920
But what they really
measure is these two.

01:16:09.920 --> 01:16:12.600
And you're comparing
apples and orange.

01:16:12.600 --> 01:16:15.540
And you see, sometimes, they
have perfect comparison.

01:16:15.540 --> 01:16:19.740
You know something's wrong, OK?

01:16:19.740 --> 01:16:22.080
But this is not just
this one problem.

01:16:22.080 --> 01:16:24.900
Actually, you want to
check the literature.

01:16:24.900 --> 01:16:27.780
It's everywhere
people discuss it.

01:16:27.780 --> 01:16:30.780
People really do this kind
of research [INAUDIBLE]..

01:16:30.780 --> 01:16:33.250
Let me give examples.

01:16:33.250 --> 01:16:40.080
And so now you can use
this to understand there

01:16:40.080 --> 01:16:42.980
are many interesting phenomena.

01:16:42.980 --> 01:16:45.870
What we discuss here is
thermal boundary resistance.

01:16:45.870 --> 01:16:48.120
That's where I'm
really interested.

01:16:48.120 --> 01:16:50.190
So I spent time on it.

01:16:50.190 --> 01:16:57.570
But say in electrons-- so
if you have a wire or a dot,

01:16:57.570 --> 01:17:01.020
people do a lot of this
kind of experiment,

01:17:01.020 --> 01:17:03.180
where they measure
the voltage here--

01:17:03.180 --> 01:17:07.370
V1 and V2-- and
go through a wire.

01:17:07.370 --> 01:17:09.258
And when the wire's
very small, it

01:17:09.258 --> 01:17:10.550
goes to a very low temperature.

01:17:10.550 --> 01:17:13.520
You got quantized conductance.

01:17:13.520 --> 01:17:16.610
And that conductance is actually
independent of material.

01:17:16.610 --> 01:17:19.150
It's universal.

01:17:19.150 --> 01:17:22.510
And same if you do heat
at a very low temperature.

01:17:22.510 --> 01:17:25.540
This is all n the book,
the example problem.

01:17:25.540 --> 01:17:27.730
You get a universal
heat conductance.

01:17:27.730 --> 01:17:32.570
It's the independent
material, OK?

01:17:32.570 --> 01:17:35.780
And then, the question
is, there was a question

01:17:35.780 --> 01:17:37.820
that often people ask.

01:17:37.820 --> 01:17:42.010
If it's a ballistic, why do
you have resistance at all?

01:17:42.010 --> 01:17:44.700
Ballistic means to go from
one point to the other.

01:17:44.700 --> 01:17:48.490
Why you have resistance at all?

01:17:48.490 --> 01:17:51.810
And you see, here is-- those
are the ballistic quantized

01:17:51.810 --> 01:17:54.780
conductance, right?

01:17:54.780 --> 01:18:03.100
And this really depends
on the definition.

01:18:03.100 --> 01:18:07.300
And in the case of your
experiment-- in the case where

01:18:07.300 --> 01:18:09.700
people do this
quantized conductance

01:18:09.700 --> 01:18:12.610
measurement, for example,
you have a carbon nanotube.

01:18:12.610 --> 01:18:14.650
You measure the conductance.

01:18:14.650 --> 01:18:18.580
You put two metal
electrodes, right?

01:18:18.580 --> 01:18:20.740
And so you have a nanotube.

01:18:20.740 --> 01:18:23.560
You put the two
metal electrodes.

01:18:23.560 --> 01:18:28.990
And what you measure is not this
temperature or electrochemical

01:18:28.990 --> 01:18:30.610
potential.

01:18:30.610 --> 01:18:33.040
You're measuring the
[INAUDIBLE] and the source.

01:18:33.040 --> 01:18:37.940
This is the average
over a large area.

01:18:37.940 --> 01:18:39.890
So what does that mean?

01:18:39.890 --> 01:18:43.220
It's what launched it into
this region corresponds

01:18:43.220 --> 01:18:44.870
to what you measure.

01:18:44.870 --> 01:18:49.250
What's launched is your--
immediately electron.

01:18:49.250 --> 01:18:50.960
If you had the chance--

01:18:50.960 --> 01:18:55.860
there is a way to measure
exactly the potential here

01:18:55.860 --> 01:18:59.810
of incoming plus ballistic
going from this side

01:18:59.810 --> 01:19:03.490
to the other side, right?

01:19:03.490 --> 01:19:07.550
Then this point and this point--
there is the same potential.

01:19:07.550 --> 01:19:12.270
If you use that same potential,
you get infinite conductance,

01:19:12.270 --> 01:19:12.770
OK?

01:19:12.770 --> 01:19:15.290
So it's really how
you-- because those are

01:19:15.290 --> 01:19:17.490
all a non-equilibrium process.

01:19:17.490 --> 01:19:20.510
It depends on how you
do your measurement, how

01:19:20.510 --> 01:19:25.740
you interpret it.

01:19:25.740 --> 01:19:29.210
So in electrical engineering, I
remember I was reading a book.

01:19:29.210 --> 01:19:32.610
There was a whole section
devoted to this discussion.

01:19:32.610 --> 01:19:34.700
And in fact, you go to check--

01:19:34.700 --> 01:19:37.022
once I was looking
into this paper.

01:19:37.022 --> 01:19:39.230
Professor Peter [INAUDIBLE]
in the physics department

01:19:39.230 --> 01:19:43.130
had a few papers on this
quantized conductance.

01:19:43.130 --> 01:19:46.640
Same problem, different fields.

01:19:46.640 --> 01:19:50.060
This is just really, you
have to spend your time

01:19:50.060 --> 01:19:52.180
to understand it.

01:19:52.180 --> 01:19:55.390
Finally, just a another
example for you to think

01:19:55.390 --> 01:19:59.830
is, when I have two
black body radiations--

01:19:59.830 --> 01:20:01.840
pretend two surfaces, right?

01:20:01.840 --> 01:20:02.650
In a vacuum--

01:20:02.650 --> 01:20:04.932
T1, T2.

01:20:04.932 --> 01:20:06.640
What's the temperature
of photons inside?

01:20:09.890 --> 01:20:10.940
There's no equilibrium.

01:20:10.940 --> 01:20:13.010
You have two
counter-propagating photons.

01:20:13.010 --> 01:20:15.460
They don't interact.

01:20:15.460 --> 01:20:19.990
Now, if you have this black
body absorber [INAUDIBLE] disk.

01:20:19.990 --> 01:20:22.240
On one side, they absorb T1.

01:20:22.240 --> 01:20:24.700
On the other side,
they absorb T2.

01:20:24.700 --> 01:20:27.130
So at the end, your
absorbed temperature

01:20:27.130 --> 01:20:32.980
will be T force equals T1
force plus T2 force average.

01:20:32.980 --> 01:20:39.600
And that temperature
is uniform here, right?

01:20:39.600 --> 01:20:41.120
But when you stick
a thermocouple,

01:20:41.120 --> 01:20:44.240
you measure this hot side,
you measure another photon

01:20:44.240 --> 01:20:46.887
temperature, you measure
solid temperature.

01:20:46.887 --> 01:20:48.470
You stick a thermocouple
on this side,

01:20:48.470 --> 01:20:50.850
you measure the
solid temperature.

01:20:50.850 --> 01:20:52.850
So when you do your
black body, you say, my q

01:20:52.850 --> 01:21:00.120
equals sigma T1 force power
minus T2 force power, right?

01:21:00.120 --> 01:21:03.540
And correspondingly, you
do your q over delta T.

01:21:03.540 --> 01:21:08.440
That's your conductance, right?

01:21:08.440 --> 01:21:10.780
It's not infinite.

01:21:10.780 --> 01:21:13.510
However, if you are able
to put this detector--

01:21:13.510 --> 01:21:15.280
measure this point
and this point,

01:21:15.280 --> 01:21:18.260
it's the same temperature.

01:21:18.260 --> 01:21:21.680
You should get a
conductance infinite, right?

01:21:21.680 --> 01:21:24.810
So it depends on
how you define it.

01:21:24.810 --> 01:21:26.560
You have to be careful.

01:21:26.560 --> 01:21:29.900
That's my point, OK?

01:21:29.900 --> 01:21:31.930
